0.14.1
---
Fixed
- The quick access bar takes the width it asks for once a style sheet has reached
  it, including a sheet installed after the window was already on the screen.
  What the bar's layout gives the quick access bar is the width of its size hint,
  and a sheet is one of the things that decides what that hint is - the shipped
  themes pad the buttons, so the bar wants more room after one lands. A sheet that
  arrived late changed the hint after the only pass that would have answered it
  had run: this class is told about the new style before the tool bar inside the
  quick access bar is, so the width it read was still the old one, and the last
  button of the bar was cut off by the widget that followed it. The request the
  tool bar posts when its own size changes is now a reason to lay the bar out
  again, next to the one the corner widgets already had.
  tst_RibbonBar::quickAccessBarFollowsASheetThatLandsLate is the check.

0.14.0
---
Addition
- RibbonPageContext::addPage(RibbonPage *) takes a page it did not build, the
  counterpart of RibbonBar::addPage(RibbonPage *). Until now a context page could
  only be the plain RibbonPage addPage(title) constructs, so a caller with its own
  RibbonPage subclass had no way into a context tab. The overload does not take
  ownership: the caller still decides when the page dies. tst_RibbonPageContext::
  builtPage is the check.
- RibbonBar::setCornerWidget(QWidget *, Qt::Corner) is the ribbon-aware way to put a
  widget in one of the bar's top corners. QMenuBar's own function cannot do it for
  this bar: a widget created without a parent arrives hidden and a hidden widget
  takes no part in the ribbon layout, and nothing asks the bar to lay itself out
  again, so the widget keeps the position and size it was born with. The ribbon's
  entry point shows it and re-runs the layout. Those corners are the ones Qt uses
  while an MDI child is maximized, for the child's icon and its window buttons.
- A zh_CN catalogue, qxribbon_zh_CN.ts next to the sources. Every user-visible
  string this library shows - tooltips and menu entries alike - is translated
  there and compiled into a qxribbon_zh_CN.qm the application loads; see the
  internationalisation page for how an application reaches it. The diagnostics
  the library writes through qDebug() are the deliberate exception: they stay
  English so that the same words can be found in a search whatever language the
  interface runs in. tst_RibbonTranslations::catalogue holds the strings to that.

Fixed
- A gallery's popup keeps one item size whatever the strip is showing, and it stays
  on the screen. Picking an item out of another group makes the strip show that
  group - that is what the Office gallery does, and the strip still shows the rows
  DisplayRow asks for - but a group asking for three display rows divides the
  strip's height by three, so its own grid is a third as tall. The popup took that
  grid as the item size for every group it showed, so each pick made the next popup
  smaller by a step, and the sizes had nothing to do with the popup's own width,
  which is where the items are laid out. An item is now one row of the strip's,
  taken from the strip's height rather than from the group the strip happens to
  show. The height a group needs is measured at the width it really gets as well:
  it used to be derived from a guessed width (the popup's minus a scroll bar and a
  fudge), which came out one column short of the width the layout then handed the
  preview and left a row of empty space under the last group -
  RibbonGalleryPreview::syncHeight() states it when the width arrives, since a
  scroll area never asks for heightForWidth. And the popup is placed against the
  screen and not the strip alone: it opens upwards when the room below is not
  enough, measured from the strip's top edge (stepping back from its bottom used to
  hang the popup off the top of the screen whenever the window sat low), it is cut
  down to the side with more room when neither side can hold it, and it is moved
  back onto the screen sideways when the gallery is nearly as wide as the screen.
  tst_RibbonGallery::popupItemsKeepTheirSize and popupStaysOnScreen are the checks.
- Corner widgets keep the room and the row the ribbon gives them. Both are outside this
  class's control - Qt installs an MDI child's icon and window buttons there, and an
  application may install anything - and the placement computed by the last layout went
  stale in two ways. The widget can ask for a different size, so a longer label, a
  different font or a show/hide left it holding the width it had while its size hint had
  moved on. And QMenuBar lays corner widgets out itself, across the full height of the
  menu bar (Qt 5 and 6, QMenuBarPrivate::updateGeometries), which is where a menu bar's
  corners belong and not where a ribbon's rows are: a maximized MDI child's icon ended up
  centred in the middle of the ribbon, well below the title row it belongs on, and the
  buttons dropped to the bar's top edge instead of the tab row. That placement gives them
  their size hint width as well, so a check on the width alone saw nothing wrong - and
  setCornerWidget() is not virtual, so QMdiArea reaches QMenuBar's implementation and
  this class never got a chance to place them first. The LayoutRequest handler now re-runs
  the placement when a corner widget is not where the ribbon put it, width or band, with
  the vertical rule shared with placeCornerWidget(). The request QMenuBar posts after
  placing them is the signal the bar gets for it. tst_RibbonBar::cornerWidgetTakesItsRoom,
  cornerWidgetArrivesAfterTheBarIsUp, cornerWidgetInstalledThroughTheMenuBar and
  maximizedMdiChildUsesTheCorners are the checks; corner widget support had no test at all
  until now, although both layout functions have always placed them.
- The application button is laid out at the tab strip's full height instead of one
  pixel less. A tab title is centred in its tab rect, and so is a label the button
  carries of its own, so a button one baseline row shorter centred that label half
  a pixel above every title beside it - a File button that visibly does not sit on
  the tabs' line. tst_RibbonBar::applicationButtonSharesTheTabRow is the check, in
  both tab-row styles.
- A quick access item whose action carries a menu pops that menu up on a click and
  no longer shows the platform style's menu triangle. The triangle is
  PM_MenuButtonIndicator wide - twelve pixels of a control twenty-two wide, the
  largest shape in a row of 16-pixel glyphs and far enough right of the glyph to
  read as a mark of its own. It cannot be dropped from a style sheet: ::menu-indicator
  takes an image, but a sheet draws that image as it stands and so cannot follow the
  palette, and a rule that names only the sub-control is passed over while the
  platform style draws the button. InstantPopup is what turns it off, and an item
  whose action has a menu has nothing of its own to trigger anyway - a click used to
  fire the action, which for a menu-only action did nothing visible, and the menu
  needed a press-and-hold. The shipped themes drop the indicator too, which is what
  covers the tables that describe these buttons themselves.
  tst_RibbonQuickAccessBar::menuItemTakesNoMoreRoom is the check.
- The ribbon's minimize button is painted from the palette, and says what it does.
  It asked the style for SP_TitleBarShadeButton, and a platform style draws that
  standard icon in black whatever the palette says, so on a dark sheet it was the
  one glyph left black on black; it also carried no tooltip, being an icon and
  nothing else. updateMinimumButtonIcon() paints the mask in ButtonText now, the
  way the gallery's scroll arrows and the group's option button already did, and
  RibbonBar::event() repaints it on PaletteChange so that a theme switch reaches it
  too. The tooltip is "Minimize the Ribbon", or "Pin the Ribbon" while minimized.
  It reads the bar's palette rather than the button's: the repaint runs from the
  bar's PaletteChange, where the bar already carries the new palette and the button
  may not have inherited it yet. tst_RibbonBar::minimumButtonFollowsThePalette is
  the check, in both directions of a palette change.
- RibbonScreenTip resolves its colours again every time it is shown, instead of
  naming palette() roles inside its style sheet. A style sheet resolves those
  roles when the widget is polished, and the tip is one instance that lives for
  the whole run, so "polished once" meant "in whatever theme happened to be up
  the first time a tip was shown": after a switch to a dark theme the tip kept
  the light theme's black text. tst_RibbonButton::screenTipFollowsThePalette is
  the check.
- Two strings finally answer to a translation. "Back", the tooltip of the
  backstage view's close button, was asked for through q->tr() and "app", the
  application button's default text, through a bare tr() - both from inside a
  private class that derives QObject without Q_OBJECT, and that is the one place
  where lupdate and the run time disagree on what a context is: lupdate files the
  message under the class it is written in, while tr() answers with the class
  whose Q_OBJECT defined it, that class having no Q_OBJECT of its own and so
  inheriting QObject's. The two names never met, which no amount of translating
  could have cured. Both now name their context explicitly, in the public class
  the string belongs to.
- The close button's hover glyph no longer reads QPalette::HighlightedText. That
  role is the pair of QPalette::Highlight - what sits on the application's
  emphasis - and a theme that emphasizes with a light colour makes it dark,
  which painted a dark glyph on this button's own fill. The fill here is not the
  emphasis but the close button's, so the glyph is white, which is what the
  button's file was drawn in and what reads on it.
- A gallery item's bottom line is inside the strip again. The grid was sized as
  the height over the rows, which is the height of a row but not the room one
  has for it: the rows have to fit between the gaps above, below and between
  them as well, so the last row reached past the widget's bottom edge and the
  bottom of its box - the line that says which item is selected - was clipped
  away, which reads as the gallery cutting the item off. The grid is
  (height - (rows + 1) * spacing) / rows now, so the last row ends where the
  strip does. tst_RibbonGallery::geometry asserts every item's bottom is inside
  the widget, alongside the check that selecting one still moves nothing.
- A caller's own application button is dressed by the theme now. The sheets
  answered to the ribbon's own object name only, and setApplicationButton()
  granted that name to a button that had none - so a button the application
  named itself kept its name and with it the platform's press look, which read
  as the button sinking where the themed ones stay flat. The sheets answer to a
  dynamic property as well now: setApplicationButton() sets
  "qxRibbonApplicationButton" on the button and re-polishes it, and every
  application-button selector in the skeletons and the family sheets answers to
  the property beside the name, each state to both. Nothing changes for an unnamed button, and a named one still keeps
  its name - only the dressing follows the button instead of the name. The
  limitations entry is gone with the boundary it described.
  tst_RibbonBar::applicationButton

Interface
- RibbonGalleryGroup is a QWidget that paints its own items, not a QListView any
  more. The view was carrying machinery the gallery never used well: a model per
  group, a delegate, a selection model, and a "mirror" group inside the gallery
  fed from the current group's model - the strip showed the copy, the popup held
  the original, and a click had to be forwarded between them. The item states
  were left to the style sheet, and the sheet could not deliver them: an ::item
  rule on a custom class matched nothing, so hover and selection were whatever
  the platform style had left behind, and the one pseudo-state that did land - a
  hover border - moved the item's contents by a pixel, which is the jumping. Now
  the group owns its items (a list of actions under one QActionGroup), computes
  its own grid, and paints from the palette: a state changes the wash and the
  border, never the geometry, and the label keeps QPalette::Text instead of
  reversing to white on a wash. The popup paints each group with the same
  painter, so the strip and the popup cannot disagree about where an item is.

  The member surface keeps its shape - the styles, the display rows, the grid
  bounds, recalcGridSize(), selectByIndex(), triggerCurrentItem(), actionGroup()
  and the three signals all answer as before - but the class it inherits is
  QWidget, and what the view leaked is gone: no model(), currentIndex(),
  scrollTo() or QListView's spacing(). In their place the group says what it now
  is: itemCount(), itemAction(), selectedIndex(), itemRect(), setSpacing(),
  gridSize(), and the viewport/scroll pair (setViewportRows(), scrollByRows(),
  scrollToItem()) the gallery drives. currentViewGroup() returns the group the
  strip shows, which is the group itself and not a copy of it - the assertion
  that the two were different objects was the old design's, and tst_RibbonGallery
  now asserts the opposite.

0.13.0
---
Interface
- RibbonBar::RibbonStyle names its four values for the layout each one selects
  rather than for the product it was modelled on: OfficeStyle is TabBarBelowStyle,
  WpsLiteStyle is TabBarInlineStyle, OfficeStyleTwoRow is TabBarBelowTwoRowStyle
  and WpsLiteStyleTwoRow is TabBarInlineTwoRowStyle. isOfficeStyle() is
  hasOwnTabRow(), which is the question it was answering all along - the four
  values are the combinations of two independent choices, whether the tab bar gets
  a row of its own below the title row and whether a group stacks its small
  buttons two rows deep or three. The names described neither choice, and the two
  that promised a shorter ribbon disagreed: OfficeStyleTwoRow and WpsLiteStyle are
  the same height, having saved their row in different places, so "Lite" read as a
  third axis rather than as the same one. The values are unchanged (0x0000,
  0x0001, and 0x0100 or those), so what breaks is the spelling a program writes
  and the string QMetaEnum returns. See doc/pages/migration.md, "4.5".

- The library no longer carries a theme of its own. RibbonTheme is gone - both the
  class and its ThemeStyle enumeration - and with it
  RibbonMainWindow::setRibbonTheme(), ribbonTheme() and loadTheme(), and the
  ribbonTheme Q_PROPERTY. What a ribbon looks like is decided by the style sheet
  that reaches the application: the window used to install one on itself in its
  constructor, and a sheet on a widget wins over the application's wherever both
  describe the same widget, so an application that asked for a dark theme kept a
  light ribbon while the rest of its window went dark. The eight bundled style
  sheets left with the class and now live outside the library, in res/themes/, for
  an application to build in and register under ids of its choosing - see
  examples/qxribbon/ribbonstyle. RibbonBar::updateRibbonTheme() stays: it is what
  to call after a style change the bar has to re-read.

- The ribbon's own glyphs are no longer named by a style sheet. The window caption
  buttons, the group launcher, the quick access customize button, the gallery
  scroll arrows and the backstage back arrow are alpha masks painted in a palette
  role - ButtonText, or WindowText for the caption - so one asset serves light and
  dark and an application theme reaches them through the palette alone. res/lighter/
  is gone with the duplicate set it held. WindowButtonGroup::setIconScale() now
  multiplies the glyph size and defaults to 1.0; it used to scale the button's own
  size, which the caption style sheet overrode, so calling it changed nothing.

- RibbonMdiArea: add RibbonMdiArea
- RibbonBackstageView: add RibbonBackstageView (QWidget)
- RibbonStatusBar: add RibbonStatusBar (QStatusBar)
- RibbonSliderPane: add RibbonSliderPane (QWidget)
- RibbonQuickAccessBar: add customizeDialogRequested and actionCustomizeDialog
- RibbonGalleryGroup: add triggerCurrentItem
- RibbonBar: add overridable addContextMenuActions
- RibbonGroup: add setReduced and isReduced

Bugfix
- The tab strip follows a theme change. Its width is not laid out but added up by
  the bar from the tabs' own widths, and nothing re-ran that sum when a style
  sheet arrived: switching from a theme that pads ::tab by 8 pixels to one that
  pads by 12 left the strip as wide as the previous theme asked for, the wider
  tabs no longer fit, and QTabBar did what it does with tabs it cannot show - it
  scrolled them, so a pair of arrows appeared on the right of the strip. RibbonBar
  now lays itself out again on QEvent::StyleChange, queued so that the tabs are
  measured under the new sheet rather than the old one.
  tst_RibbonBar::tabBarFollowsTheTheme

- The ribbon's placeholder tab takes no width. With no page selected - the state a
  minimized ribbon is in - the bar has to select a tab that paints nothing,
  because QTabBar::setCurrentIndex(-1) does nothing at all: Qt stores an index only
  when it resolves to a tab, so the current index can be -1 only while the bar
  holds no tab. That placeholder is an empty, disabled tab at the end of the strip,
  and its width was whatever the style sheet gives ::tab - 16 to 28 pixels in the
  bundled themes - which the strip counted into its minimum width and the bar kept
  as a gap. RibbonTabBar now returns zero from tabSizeHint() and
  minimumTabSizeHint(), so the placeholder costs nothing under any theme, and
  nothing about it rests on the theme keeping ::tab transparent - it had to keep
  the selected state transparent too, which a theme that paints ::tab with a
  background would not. The wheel guard that kept the wheel off the placeholder is
  gone as well: Qt has routed the wheel through setCurrentNextEnabledIndex(), which
  stops only at enabled and visible tabs, since 5.15.
  tst_RibbonBar::noSelectionCostsNoRoom

- RibbonBackstageView's back arrow was loaded from the light asset whatever the
  palette, so on a dark theme it was a black glyph on a dark column. It is painted
  from the palette now and repainted when the palette changes.
- The KeyTip letter pool was filled with QList<QString>(int), a constructor only
  Qt 6 has, so the ribbon did not compile against Qt 5. The list is resized
  instead, which both versions provide.
- RibbonScreenTip built its look from a hard-coded light style sheet (background
  #fafafa, a 1px #adadad border, #202020/#404040/#505050 text, a #ffffff shortcut
  box), so on a dark application theme it still painted a white box. It now reads
  every colour from the palette - background and shortcut box from tooltipbase,
  text from tooltiptext, borders from mid - which re-resolves when the
  application palette changes.
- RibbonManager::setupFactory() never leaves the manager without a factory.
  Passing null used to store null, and every ribbon element created after that
  goes through the RibbonElementFactory macro - which dereferences what
  factory() returns - so uninstalling the factory crashed the next element the
  library made, WindowButtonGroup included. Null now puts the default factory
  back, and passing the factory that is already installed is a no-op rather
  than deleting the one it was about to store.
  tst_RibbonFactoryAndManager::setupFactoryNullPutsTheDefaultBack

Cleanup
- The scroll offset of a ribbon page is clamped in one place. The legal range
  was written down four times - a pair of hand-rolled if-chains in the wheel
  path, a qBound() in the button path, and twice more in the layout pass - which
  agreed only because someone had read them against each other. They now all
  fold into RibbonPagePrivate::clampXBase(), so a path that moves the content
  cannot answer "how far" differently from its neighbours. No behaviour change.
  tst_RibbonPage::wheelAndButtonsShareOneRange drives both paths to both ends
  and compares them pixel for pixel, which is the check the duplication made
  impossible to write before.
- RibbonGalleryGroupItemDelegate loses the two paint helpers that held nothing.
  paintIconWithText() and paintIconWithTextWordWrap() were one line each, and that
  line was the call to QStyledItemDelegate::paint() the surrounding switch reaches
  for anyway; the two text presentations now make that call directly, and only
  paintIconOnly() - the one presentation that paints something the base does not -
  keeps a helper of its own. The delegate is private to the library, so no caller
  outside it could have overridden the pair. No behaviour change.
- RibbonButton::actionEvent() is gone, an override whose entire body was the call
  to QToolButton::actionEvent(). The base implementation is reached directly now.
  A subclass that overrides actionEvent() keeps working: the slot it overrides is
  QWidget's, not the removed one's. No behaviour change.
- The HELP_DRAW_RECT macro in ribbongroup.cpp was defined unconditionally while
  its two call sites sit inside #ifdef QX_RIBBON_DEBUG_HELP_DRAW, so every ordinary
  build carried a macro it never expanded. The definition now sits under the same
  guard as the one in ribbonbutton.cpp, which keeps the opt-in layout aid intact
  and the two files consistent.
- RibbonActionsManagerModelPrivate::m_seatchText is spelled m_searchText. The
  member records the text the model is filtered by; the class is private to its
  cpp, so the rename reaches nothing outside it.

Feature
- RibbonBar::resizeRibbonNow() does the layout resizeRibbon() asks for before it
  returns, instead of on the next event loop pass. resizeRibbon() defers for a
  reason (see doc/pages/limitations.md, "RibbonBar resize is deferred") and the
  deferral is observable: the caller has already changed something the user is
  looking at, and the previous geometry stays on screen for that pass. The two
  callers inside this class that needed it now - setMinimized() and
  showMinimumButton() - built the event themselves; that idiom now has a name, and
  RibbonMainWindow forwards it for callers that hold the window rather than the
  bar. tst_RibbonAppWindow::flagsChangeLaysTheGroupsOutImmediately
- KeyTips: pressing Alt labels the application button and the visible pages with
  letter badges; picking a page's letter switches to it and descends one level to
  label that page's buttons - a button's mnemonic where it has one, a generated
  A-Z letter otherwise. A control runs on click, Esc or Alt leaves the mode, and a
  resize puts the badges away. RibbonGallery is a KeyTip target too: its letter
  moves focus into the gallery, which then takes over the keyboard.
- RibbonButton shows a rich ScreenTip on hover: the action's text without its
  mnemonic, its statusTip, and its shortcut, in a shared frameless tool window
  that follows the pointer. An action with neither a statusTip nor a shortcut
  keeps the plain tooltip.
- RibbonQuickAccessBar: the customize menu now ends with the Office-style
  "Customize Quick Access Toolbar..." entry, after a separator, which emits the
  new customizeDialogRequested() signal for the host to open its dialog. The entry
  action is owned by the bar rather than the menu, because the menu clears itself
  when it hides and would delete an action it owned.
- RibbonGallery is keyboard reachable: it takes focus and handles the arrow keys
  (moving the current item, clamped at the ends), Enter/Space (activating the
  current item) and PageUp/PageDown (the two scroll buttons).
- RibbonBar: right-clicking opens a context menu with "Minimize the Ribbon"
  (checkable, tracking isMinimized()) and the quick access bar's customize entry.
  Subclasses can extend it by overriding addContextMenuActions().
- RibbonPage and RibbonGroup mirror their title into accessibleName (mnemonics
  stripped, kept in step with windowTitleChanged) and the ribbon bar carries a
  stable name; a QAccessible factory gives the painted page and group containers
  the Pane and Grouping roles, which their custom painting otherwise hid from
  assistive tools.
- A page that runs out of room narrows its groups before it falls back to its
  scroll buttons. A group has a reduced presentation in which a button that would
  own a whole column is packed as a small one instead, trading rows for width; the
  page reduces groups from the right until they fit, so a page with room always
  shows every group at full width, and the left and right scroll buttons appear
  only when the narrowest form of every group still overflows.
- RibbonMdiArea is a tabbed-document MDI area that keeps the ribbon in step
  with the active document. Documents open as tabs by default (tabbed view
  plus document mode, closable and movable), and a document may carry a
  RibbonPage that is shown while the document is active and hidden while it
  is not, so switching tabs swaps the ribbon pages - the treatment a ribbon
  application gives its documents. Of the pages it manages, only the active
  document's stays up, and none at all while nothing is active; a document
  added without a page leaves the ribbon alone, and so does a page that is on
  no RibbonBar. The area paints nothing of its own - the document tabs are the
  plain tab bar QMdiArea drives - so it adds no visual semantics a theme would
  have to follow. tst_RibbonMdiArea drives the swap, the close paths and the
  guards on both legs.

- RibbonBackstageView: add the full-takeover backstage panel the application button
  opens - Office's File menu as a panel that covers the ribbon bar's host and lays
  the document commands down a left column and their pages to the right of it.
  addAction()/addSeparator()/addPage() fill the column; setActivePage()/activePage()
  swap the right-hand page; open() shows the panel and Esc or the styled close
  button hides it, unless setClosePrevented() holds it open; the panel answers
  RibbonBar::applicationButtonClicked by itself, so a host that wants it to do
  something else disconnects that signal. The column, its button and its separator
  carry the qx_RibbonBackstage* object names, so a theme styles them the way it
  styles the rest of the ribbon, and the panel paints nothing of its own - the
  column hugs the left edge and the page fills what is left. tst_RibbonBackstageView
  drives open/close, the page swap, the host-resize follow and the application-button
  toggle; Example_Ribbon_Backstage is a host that ends in the backstage.

- RibbonStatusBar: add the ribbon-styled status bar (QStatusBar). A host puts
  plain-text segments on the left and view switches on the right the way Office's
  ribbon status bar does. addAction() adds a left segment the host updates by
  setting the action's text, addSeparator() adds a hairline between two of them,
  and addPermanentAction() adds a right-hand switch that stays put while
  showMessage() scrolls its text across the left. The bar carries the
  qx_RibbonStatusBar object name and paints nothing of its own, so a dark theme
  styles it the way it styles the rest of the ribbon - the dark themes already
  carry a QStatusBar rule; the segments and the permanent buttons carry their own
  qx_RibbonStatusBar* names. tst_RibbonStatusBar drives the two sections, the text
  tracking and the showMessage() hide; Example_Ribbon_StatusBar is a host with
  both sections filled.

- RibbonSliderPane: add the Office zoom control - a minus button, a slider track
  and a plus button in one compact pane, the control a ribbon application puts at
  the right end of its status bar. The buttons step by singleStep (auto-repeat on
  hold), the track reports valueChanged() when the level settles and sliderMoved()
  for every step of a drag; setRange() bounds both and setScrollButtons(false)
  keeps the track alone. It is a plain widget, so a host joins it to the permanent
  section with QStatusBar::addPermanentWidget() and a message never pushes it out.
  Nothing paints here either: the stock widgets carry the qx_RibbonSliderPane*
  object names and the buttons draw their glyphs from the palette, so they read on
  a dark theme without one carrying icons. tst_RibbonSliderPane drives the range,
  the buttons and the forwarding of both signals.

- ribbonutils: add tintedPixmap() and tintedIcon(). A monochrome asset is a
  coverage mask, so painting it in a palette colour is a SourceIn fill: the alpha
  keeps the glyph's shape and antialiasing while the colour is replaced, which is
  how one asset serves every theme. The ribbon uses them for its own glyphs; an
  application with mask art of its own can use them for the same purpose.
  tintedIcon() rasterises the asset once per device pixel ratio QIcon may ask for,
  so a vector mask stays sharp on a high dpi screen.

0.12.2
---
Cleanup
- Drop the Qt 5.7 branches in RibbonGridContainerPrivate::init() and
  RibbonCustomizeWidget::initConnection(). Both guarded the QOverload spelling
  of the connect() calls against the static_cast spelling, and the threshold is
  below the 5.15 minimum, so the QOverload form is now used unconditionally.

Bugfix
- RibbonPage scrolled to the right end and then widened left a gap as wide as
  the resize between the last group and the right edge, with the scroll buttons
  still reporting the right end as reached. The page clamps its horizontal
  offset only when the content stops overflowing, so an offset that was legal
  for the narrower page stayed behind the raised lower bound of its legal range
  and the content stopped short of the viewport. The offset is now clamped
  against the width the layout has just measured - the size hint total carries
  the contents margins and none of the expanding width, so clamping with that
  would land on the wrong pixel - and the geometry of the same pass is shifted
  by the amount the offset moved instead of being measured twice. Clicking a
  scroll button healed the gap before, which made it a transient visual defect
  rather than corrupted state.
- The window button group came up in the top-left corner, over the tab bar,
  and only moved to the top-right after the window was resized by hand. The
  ribbon lays itself out through resizeRibbon(), which queues a resize event,
  and a window is normally still hidden when that event is delivered: the
  AppShell demo drains the queue through QxSplashScreen::step() while the
  window is being built. Every layout decision in the four resize and
  minimumWidth functions asked isVisible(), which is false for the whole
  window at that point, so the group was skipped and left at its default
  position, and nothing re-ran the layout until the window happened to be
  resized. Those functions now ask whether a widget is explicitly hidden,
  through one helper, so the first layout - the one that runs while the
  window is hidden - produces the geometry the first painted frame shows.
  The paint functions keep isVisible(): they only run while the window is
  visible and they ask the other question.
- WindowButtonGroupPrivate::sizeHint() was the second half of the same
  defect: it summed the button widths from isVisible(), so a group sized
  while the window was hidden came out zero wide. Fixing the ribbon alone
  would therefore have placed a zero-width group on the title bar with its
  buttons stacked on top of each other. The size hint now reads the hidden
  flag as well.

0.12.1
---
Bugfix
- RibbonCustomizeWidget recorded a "move down" on an action as a "move up": the
  action branch handed -1 to makeChangeActionOrderCustomizeData(), and on that
  function -1 means up while 1 means down - which is what the page and group
  branches pass. apply() moves the action to idx + indexValue, so the recorded
  change moved it the wrong way.
- The ribbon bar named its tab bar twice, so the second name clobbered the first:
  the tab bar ended up called "qx_RibbonStackedWidget" and the stacked widget had
  no name at all. The stacked widget now carries that name and the tab bar keeps
  "qx_RibbonTabBar". No shipped stylesheet refers to either name today, so nothing
  visible changes - but the names are part of what a caller can target from its
  own stylesheet, and the bar was answering wrongly for both.
- RibbonPage left two of its private fields uninitialised. RibbonPagePrivate's
  constructor set the page's cached content width and its horizontal scroll
  offset nowhere, so a wheel event or a scroll button click that arrived before
  the first layout pass compared and clamped an indeterminate value. Both now
  start at 0 - the value the first layout pass gives a page with no content.

Cleanup
- RibbonCustomizeWidget no longer declares isChanged() and model(). Nothing in
  the tree called either one. The class is not exported, and the limitations page
  records the dialog that hosts it as the whole interface, so both accessors were
  answering for state the class keeps to itself: the ribbon tree view and the
  item look-ups already reach the model directly.
- Six places posted the deferred resize by hand - a line-for-line copy of what
  RibbonBar::resizeRibbon() already does - so the reason the resize is queued
  rather than run in place was written down in one place and repeated in six.
  They all call resizeRibbon() now, which makes it the single entry point for
  "the ribbon changed shape, ask for a resize". No behaviour change: the posted
  event is identical.

0.12.0
---
Bugfix
- RibbonTheme::setTheme(RibbonTheme::CustomTheme) dereferenced the widget pointer
  it was handed, which made the documented way of calling it - the widget argument
  defaults to nullptr - a crash on a null pointer. CustomTheme now clears the
  application stylesheet when there is no widget, which is what loadTheme() has
  always done for a nullptr (A7)
- RibbonCtrlContainer built with Qt::Vertical centred its icon while one switched
  to Qt::Vertical afterwards left-aligned it: init() set the alignment without
  looking at the orientation, and only setOrientation() applied the rule. Both
  paths now start from the same one (A7)

- QX_RIBBON_DEPRECATED_SINCE(major, minor) never answered true for a deprecation
  that had already happened: QX_RIBBON_DISABLE_DEPRECATED_BEFORE defaulted to
  QX_RIBBON_VERSION, so the deprecation point was compared against the version
  being built - (0,11,0) > (0,11,0) is false - and the answer flipped back to
  false on every minor bump. The default is now a fixed floor, so an API stays
  declared for as long as it is inside its removal buffer (K26), and raising the
  cutoff is what compiles it out. tst_qxdeprecated covers all seven libraries

Interface
- FramelessHelper and RibbonWindow are removed (deprecated in 4.2, K27 / K26).
  FramelessHelper was the frameless backend RibbonWindow carried so that qxribbon
  needs no dependency on qxwindow: Qt::FramelessWindowHint plus simulated hit
  testing, move and resize, with a native path on Windows under Qt 5. Its Qt 6
  path - and, outside Windows, every path - is the incomplete half, where a window
  shown maximised for the first time cannot be restored; see the limitations page.
  RibbonWindow is RibbonMainWindow plus that backend and nothing else, so the two
  went together rather than leaving an empty shell behind. On every Qt version the
  answer is QxApp::RibbonAppWindow, which frames the same RibbonMainWindow through
  qxwindow; on Qt 5 that costs a dependency on qxapp, and qxwindow's native
  backend does build there. Every sample moves to RibbonMainWindow. See
  doc/pages/migration.md, section "4.3 已删除"

- RibbonGalleryViewport loses QX_RIBBON_EXPORT. It lives in ribbongallery_p.h and
  was privatized on purpose; only the export macro still claimed otherwise. Every
  .h in a library's SOURCES is installed, private ones included, so the header
  was reachable on disk without the class ever being public API - a caller that
  wants its own gallery popup overrides RibbonGallery::showMoreTrigger() and
  never names this class. It is the only exported class in a _p.h in the tree

Cleanup
- Remove the Qt version branch below 5.15 in FramelessWidgetDataNativeWin::devicePixelRatio,
  which K1 set as the minimum; QWidget::screen() has been the way to reach the
  screen since then

0.11.0
---
Deprecated
- FramelessHelper: it is the frameless backend of RibbonWindow, and on Windows
  with Qt 5 it is the working path; everywhere else - including every Qt 6
  platform - the path it implements is the incomplete half, where a window
  shown maximised for the first time cannot be restored. It is marked with
  QT_DEPRECATED_X and points at QxApp::RibbonAppWindow, which goes through
  qxwindow. RibbonWindow itself is unchanged and still uses it. Removal needs
  at least one release of warning first (K26).

Added
- RibbonStyleOption: add setBaseHeight(), setTitleBarHeight() and setTabBarHeight()
  so the ribbon size layout can be tuned without subclassing; recalc() keeps an
  explicit title/tab and only recomputes the four derived ribbon-bar heights

Cleanup
- RibbonButton: drop the stale "todo" in the FontChange handler - it asked for
  exactly what the next three lines already do, and sizeHint() recomputes its
  cached value on every call
- RibbonStyleOption: drop the "@todo define the ribbon layout in a config file";
  the extension point it described already exists - the class is virtual and
  RibbonFactory::setRibbonStyleOption() takes a subclass - and a config file is a
  caller-side policy rather than a gap here

0.10.2
---
Bugfix
- RibbonMainWindow: keep the window at least as wide as the ribbon bar needs
- RibbonGridContainer: make enterEvent an override on Qt 6, where its parameter
  is QEnterEvent *; the Qt 5 signature hid the virtual there and the handler
  never ran, which left a menu with two entries looking current

